<ReflectionLayout lang="en" title="Who Does the Thinking?" subtitle="AI help, Bible study and the parts of faith we shouldn’t hand over.">
 <p>It is late on a Saturday. A small-group leader has a passage for tomorrow evening, Luke 10:38–42, and a week that left no room to prepare. She opens an assistant and asks for help. Within seconds it offers a summary of the passage, three points about what it means, six discussion questions and a closing prayer. All of it is reasonable. Some of it is good. She could print it and go to bed.</p>
 <p>Nothing about this scene is scandalous, and she isn’t lazy. Christians have always leaned on help to understand the Bible. Yet something has changed hands. Before she read the passage, someone else said what it means. Before she prayed about the people in her group, a prayer was ready for her to read. The question this essay asks is simple to state and harder to answer: in moments like this, who does the thinking, and who meets God in the text?</p>

 <h2>Most help is access</h2>
 <p>Start with what shouldn’t worry us. Bible study mostly involves reading. A searchable Bible, a second translation one tap away, cross-references, a map, a word study on the Greek behind a verse, an audio recording for the commute, a commentary open beside the text: none of this takes away a skill the reader needs. The person still reads, weighs what they find and responds. Calling this de-skilling would be wrong, and it would sound like nostalgia for paper.</p>
 <p>Help with the text is as old as the text. When the Law was read to the people gathered in Jerusalem, the Levites read from it, “explaining it and giving insight, so that the people could understand what was being read” (Nehemiah 8:8). The Ethiopian official reading Isaiah asked, “How can I … unless someone guides me?” (Acts 8:31). Our <a href={`${base}/patterns/study-helps/`}>study-helps pattern</a> puts the test in one line: good help sends the reader back to the verse with more than they came with.</p>
 <p>Christopher Noessel, a designer who has worked on AI at IBM, makes the same point from the other side. In <em>Designing Assistant Technology</em> he warns against overapplying his own advice: “Not Everything Needs Learning” (Ch. 11). Drivers don’t need to memorize every route, and nobody should try to memorize the traffic. He borrows Stewart Brand’s idea of pace layers: slow-changing things are worth internalizing; fast-changing things can be looked up every time. For a Bible app, that sorts much of the work. A verse reference, a translation note or a reading-plan schedule can simply be found. Scripture itself, the language of prayer and a church’s teaching change slowly, and many Christians want to carry them.</p>

 <h2>Where help becomes a substitute</h2>
 <p>The concern is narrower than convenience. Help becomes a substitute when it does the person’s own part of a practice: the part that exists to form them or to bring them before God. It states what a passage means before the person has read it. It composes the prayer the person then only reads aloud. It produces the study or the sermon “ready to preach.”</p>
 <p>Stefanie Hutka’s <em>What Your Machines Should Do</em> begins with the question that kept coming back to her as she worked with generative AI: “Will this technology think with you or simply think for you?” (Introduction). In her chapter on thinking with technology, the human-factors researcher John D. Lee separates “core tasks,” which are core to a person’s expertise, from “chore tasks,” the drudgery that doesn’t matter (Ch. 8). For a Bible study leader, finding the passage and formatting a handout are chores. Reading it, noticing what it says and asking what it asks of her group are the core.</p>
 <p>Hutka’s own example makes the difference concrete. One AI tool for lawyers flags risky clauses, and the lawyer decides what to do with each one. Another drafts a whole contract from a short prompt, and the lawyer’s expertise “is reduced to reviewing the entire document and approving.” A third won’t generate anything until the lawyer has supplied the context and structure, which moves judgment to the start of the task (Ch. 8). The Saturday study sits squarely in the second case. And her warning applies directly: “If the affordances invite wholesale offloading by default, assume that users will accept the invitation” (Ch. 8). A tired leader will take the finished study if it’s offered first.</p>

