 <h2>What a lost route can teach</h2>
 <p>Noessel’s book opens with a small experiment he ran at IBM. About a dozen employees took a virtual commute, built from Google Maps, every day for a working week. One group had turn-by-turn directions for four days and then “lost their phones” on the fifth; their decisions at each turn took 48% longer. A second group was invited on day three to find the way themselves, with feedback after each decision. On day five, without any help, their decisions were 19% quicker than the day before (Ch. 1–2). Noessel is candid about the limits: the recordings were later lost, and he calls the numbers a ballpark. It is an illustration, not proof. But it shows the shape of the choice. The same assistant can leave someone dependent or make them more capable, depending on when it helps.</p>
 <p>His conclusion is a sentence product teams should keep: “usable is not enough” (Ch. 2). A tool can work flawlessly and still leave a person no wiser. From the experiment he builds a pattern he calls Human Goes First: an invitation, the person does the task, the result is compared with the assistant’s, and the assistant points out what the person missed (Ch. 11). He is equally candid about the cost: “What feels good and what works well are at odds here” (Ch. 12). The study he draws on, by Zana Buçinca and colleagues, asked 199 people to make decisions with an AI that was sometimes wrong. Designs that made them think before accepting its suggestion helped them catch more of its mistakes, though overall accuracy was no higher than with a plainly explained answer, and people rated least favorably the designs that cut over-reliance most.</p>
 <p>Josh Clark’s <em>Sentient Design</em> comes from a more enthusiastic place and lands nearby. Respectful interfaces, he writes, “make users more capable, not more dependent” (Ch. 1). And on deciding what to hand to an AI agent: “Sometimes the effort is the point” (Ch. 7).</p>

 <h2>The sermon is the hard case</h2>
 <p>Preparing a sermon shows why this matters most clearly. A congregation receives more than the preacher’s words on Sunday. It receives the fruit of someone having sat with the text through the week: reading it again, being puzzled by it, praying it, bringing it alongside the people they know. Isaiah’s servant says, “The Lord GOD has given Me the tongue of discipleship, to sustain the weary with a word. He awakens Me morning by morning; He awakens My ear to listen as a disciple” (Isaiah 50:4). The tongue that sustains follows the ear that has listened. Ezra “had set his heart to study the Law of the LORD, to practice it, and to teach” (Ezra 7:10), in that order. This doesn’t make the preacher’s experience the subject of the sermon; the Catholic <em>Homiletic Directory</em> warns that a homily “should not be taken up with the preacher’s personal witness” (§6). The listening shapes the preaching without becoming its topic.</p>
 <p>Pope Francis says the same thing to Catholic preachers: “Whoever wants to preach must be the first to let the word of God move him deeply and become incarnate in his daily life” (<em>Evangelii Gaudium</em> §150). Dietrich Bonhoeffer told the young pastors he trained at Finkenwalde much the same in 1936: “I cannot interpret Scripture for others if I do not let it speak to me daily.” In his homiletics lectures, as his students wrote them down, preparation begins with prayer and meditation, “personally taking in the text, word by word,” not hunting for new ideas, and the preacher renounces “all purposes of my own” so that the Word itself is heard. He didn’t ask preachers to disappear; they serve as themselves, but with “the greatest awe and restraint before the Word.” A sermon generated “ready to preach” may be accurate and well shaped. What it can’t contain is that listening. Outsourcing it changes who has been with God in the passage.</p>
 <p>The usual answer is that the preacher will review the draft. The research these books gather suggests caution. Noessel asks of every assisted step, “What happens if the AI is wrong here and the human doesn’t catch it?” (Ch. 11). In a sermon, a subtle error reaches a whole congregation. Hutka explains why review is weaker than it sounds: someone who didn’t make the decisions in a finished document lacks the context to judge them, which human-factors researchers call out-of-the-loop unfamiliarity (Ch. 8). She also reports a 2024 meta-analysis of 106 experiments by Michelle Vaccaro, Abdullah Almaatouq and Thomas Malone: on average, people working with AI did better than people alone, but in decision tasks the pair usually did worse than whichever of the two was better on its own (Ch. 9). A human signing off is not automatically a safeguard.</p>

 <h2>Preachers have always borrowed</h2>
 <p>There is a serious objection here, and it deserves a fair hearing. Preachers have always used other people’s words. Augustine, who cared deeply about preaching, allowed that someone with a good delivery but no gift for composing could memorize and preach what others had written well, and wasn’t to blame if he did so without deception (<em>On Christian Doctrine</em> IV.29). The Church of England issued its first Book of Homilies in 1547 to end “contention … through diversity of preaching” and so that “all curates, of what learning soever they be” had approved sermons to read aloud; later canons told ministers not licensed to preach to read them “without glossing or adding.” Luther’s Church Postil gave pastors model sermons for the Sundays of the church year. Today there are lectionary aids, commentaries, published sermons and homily services. The gospel itself arrives as received words: “what I received I passed on to you” (1 Corinthians 15:3).</p>
 <p>Traditions also weigh preparation differently. The Catholic <em>Homiletic Directory</em> (2014) devotes a section to the homilist’s preparation in study and prayer and commends praying the readings in the manner of lectio divina. <em>Evangelii Gaudium</em> asks for “a prolonged time of study, prayer, reflection and pastoral creativity” (§145). Lutheran and Reformed traditions have put particular weight on the preacher’s own exegesis of the text; the Westminster Directory (1645) assumes the minister’s learning will be used “in his private preparations.” Orthodox preachers often read the Fathers before they speak, deliberately preaching within a received interpretation rather than an original one. Free-church preachers often value their own wrestling with the text and speak with few notes. These differences are real, and a product shouldn’t pretend one of them is the only faithful way. The most direct church word on AI so far is Catholic: in February 2026 Pope Leo XIV urged the clergy of Rome to resist “the temptation to prepare homilies with Artificial Intelligence,” comparing the mind to a muscle that dies if it isn’t used, and saying that “to give a true homily is to share faith,” which AI “will never be able to” do (as reported by Vatican News).</p>
 <p>What the objection establishes is important: originality was never the test. Borrowed words can carry true preaching. But look at what accompanied the borrowing. Augustine’s conditions were that the words were true, that nothing was passed off deceptively, and that the one delivering them lived them; he asked such preachers to pray for those who composed the words and for their own delivery (IV.29–30). The homilies were set and answered for by the church. A postil had a known author whose teaching could be weighed. In each case the borrowing was open, someone answered for the words, and the one reading them aloud still came before God with them.</p>
 <p>A generated sermon differs on both counts. It is assembled from many people’s words, but nobody stands behind it, and it is shaped to this week and this congregation so completely that it can replace preparation instead of informing it. Prayer offers a parallel. Christians pray received words all the time, beginning with the words Jesus gave: “Our Father in heaven” (Matthew 6:9). As Alastair Roberts puts it, “Set prayers are there to be prayed. The words must become our words.” The Bishop of South Sydney, Michael Stead, finds AI useful for “turning a list of prayer points into flowing text,” but warns that composing prayers from scratch “bypasses the important task of prayerfully considering what to pray for.” Our page on <a href={`${base}/patterns/ai-spiritual-acts/`}>spiritual acts and AI</a> sets out that line in detail. The person’s part isn’t the composing. It is the attending.</p>
 <p>None of this is a rule against help in a hard week. A bivocational pastor whose child was sick all Saturday night may reach for a published sermon, and a congregation can receive it with gratitude. Dallas Willard’s line is a good guard on both sides: “Grace is not opposed to effort, it is opposed to earning.” Preparation doesn’t earn God’s favor, and a borrowed sermon isn’t a failure of faith. Grace frees us for the work of listening; it doesn’t make that work unnecessary.</p>

 <h2>Designing for the person’s part</h2>
 <p>So where does this leave a team building AI help for Bible study, prayer or preaching? For access and logistics, the machine can go first: search, translate, schedule, summarize what someone might read next. For the person’s own part, consider letting the person go first. Show the passage before any commentary. Ask what they noticed before offering an interpretation. Help a preacher test a reading, check a historical claim or find what they might have missed, and offer feedback on their draft rather than a finished one. Draft a prayer from someone’s own words, marked as a draft, instead of handing one over. Our <a href={`${base}/patterns/ai-citations/`}>citations pattern</a> includes the option of giving no generated answer at all, so readers meet the text directly.</p>
 <p>Defaults carry the weight here, because people take the first finished thing offered. They also come with an honest cost: Noessel expects people to rate person-first help lower, so a team has to judge it by what it does for people over time as well as by how it feels in the moment. Some people need more help composing, because of disability, a second language or simple exhaustion, and they deserve it without being made to feel lesser. The aim is a choice that keeps the person’s part within reach, not a test they must pass.</p>
 <p>In my paper, <a href={`${base}/research/digital-spiritual-formation/`}><em>Digital Spiritual Formation in Post-Christian Europe</em></a>, I put a graduation question to any faith product: if someone no longer needed the app, would we celebrate? I also suggested that greater practice autonomy is one sign of formation worth watching for (§2.4–2.5). That doesn’t mean everyone should stop using tools; an audio Bible or a concordance can serve for a lifetime. It means asking whether, after a season with our product, a leader reads a passage more attentively and a preacher listens more closely, or whether they have learned to wait for the draft.</p>
