 <h2>A question for your team</h2>
 <p>Take one AI feature your team offers or is planning, and one practice it touches. Write down the person’s own part of that practice in a sentence: reading, praying, preparing to teach, discerning, remembering. Then ask three questions together:</p>
 <ul>
  <li>Is this step the person’s own part, or a means to it, like finding, formatting or scheduling?</li>
  <li>Does our help support that step or replace it? What does the screen offer first?</li>
  <li>What happens if the AI is wrong and the person doesn’t catch it, and who else is affected?</li>
 </ul>
 <p>If you serve a church, try a smaller version this week: before opening any tool, have whoever is preparing write down what they heard in the passage. Then let the tools help.</p>
 <p>Back at her kitchen table, the small-group leader doesn’t need a lecture on effort. She might need an assistant that shows her the passage, asks what she notices about Mary sitting at Jesus’ feet “listening to His message” (Luke 10:39), helps with the questions she can’t answer and drafts discussion questions from her own notes. She would still be in bed before midnight. And tomorrow her group would hear from someone who had listened first.</p>

